he ones at 768×576 with ~100-190KB ARE photorealistic AI-generated images.\n\nSo the design language for Haustierzentrum seems to be:\n1. **Main hero images** (768×576): Photorealistic AI photos of the animals\n2. **Inline/extra images** (800×500 or 1024×768, tiny): Vector/illustration style - probably simple colored backgrounds with animal silhouettes or flat illustrations\n\nBut wait - the user said \"wir haben eine eigene designsprache bei den bildern, das entspricht garnicht der sprache\" - meaning the image I generated doesn't match their design language at all.\n\nAnd also \"bei golden retriever auch kein bild\" - even the golden retriever article doesn't have its proper image.\n\nSo the question is: what IS the actual design language? \n\nLooking at the data pattern more carefully:\n- The tiny files (6-8KB) seem to be SVG-style/vector illustrations with mostly flat colors\n- The large files (100-190KB) are photorealistic\n- There are some articles (9 total) that DON'T have their own hero image image file\n\nThe user wants me to figure out what the design language actually is. Since vision_analyze is rate-limited, let me look at the actual image content through other means - maybe there's a style guide somewhere or I can look at the actual pixel content.\n\nLet me try another approach - I can use Python with PIL/Pillow to analyze the images for color patterns, or I can generate a few sample images via the local queue to see the style that was used before.\n\nActually, let me look at the local-gen-queue and see what model is being used and what prompts were used for the haustier-zentrum images. Let me also check if there are older PNG versions of the images (I noticed deutscher-schaeferhund_00001_.png exists, for example).\n\nAlso, I should check if there's a style guide or documentation for the design language. Let me check memory and the skill files.",
