age.py with \"Pop Art Comic\" style\n- But the images in the folder are the gradient+silhouette style\n\nSo either:\n1. The gradient+silhouette WAS the intended style all along and the user is confused\n2. The furry/pop-art comic images were supposed to be there but were replaced by something else\n3. The user wants furry style and the gradient+silhouette is wrong\n\nThe user is saying: \"No, we had chosen a furry style\" - meaning the gradient+silhouette generated images are wrong, and they want furry/pop-art comic style instead.\n\nLet me look at what the old `generate_blog_image.py` produces vs what I generated. The session search mentions \"Pop Art Comic\" with vibrant colors, high contrast, illustrative - that's the furry/pop-art style.\n\nBut looking at the actual file sizes... the PNG source files from Jun 4 are TINY (6-8 KB for 1024x768 PNG). That's incredibly small - almost certainly vector/solid-color minimalist graphics. A pop-art comic illustration would be much larger (hundreds of KB).\n\nSo maybe the workflow was:\n1. Original images were done in pop-art comic style via ComfyUI\n2. Then someone (or another cron job) converted them to the gradient+silhouette style\n3. The user wants the pop-art comic \"furry\" style back\n\nActually wait - let me look at the file dates more carefully:\n- `golden-retriever_00001_.png` - Jun 4 14:03 \n- `deutscher-schaeferhund_00001_.png` - Jun 4 14:03\n- `maine-coon-katze_00001_.png` - Jun 4 14:03\n- `meerschweinchen-haltung_00001_.png` - Jun 4 14:03\n\nThese were ALL created at Jun 4 14:03. That's when something ran and created these gradient-style images. The WebP versions of these images show up earlier at Jun 4 14:16.\n\nThe golden retriever article itself (the HTML) had no featured image at all - it had an SVG placeholder. But the image file `golden-retriever_00001_.png` exists in the images folder.\n\nLet me look at which articles reference which images. The `_00001_` pattern (with underscore at the end) seems different from the `_00001` (no trailing underscore) pattern.\n\nLooking at the image naming patterns:\n- Style A: `slug_00001_.webp` (with underscore suffix) - larger files, 100-190KB - these are the gradient/silhouette style\n- Style B: `slug_00001.webp` (no trailing underscore) - tiny files, 6-8KB - these might be converted/webp versions\n\nWait no, looking again:\n- `hunde-gesundheit_00001_.webp` = 154,306 bytes (large, photorealistic?)\n- `golden-retriever_00001_.webp` = 7,420 bytes (tiny)\n- `golden-retriever_00001_.png` = 8,604 bytes (tiny, gradient style)\n\nBoth the PNG and WebP of golden-retriever_00001_ are tiny. And the pixel grid showed a gradient background with a white ellipse. That IS the currently deployed style.\n\nBut the user says this is wrong - they chose a \"furry style.\" So the gradient images that I was trying to match were actually already wrong/incomplete, and the user's intended design was something else entirely - a furry/pop-art comic style.\n\nI think what happened is:\n1. The original design was supposed to be furry/pop-art comic (generated via ComfyUI/generate_blog_image.py)\n2. At some point, gradient+silhouette images were put in as placeholders or by a different process\n3. The user never explicitly told me what style to use, so I imitated what I saw in the images folder\n4. The user is now correcting me: they wanted furry style\n\nSo now I need to:\n1. Acknowledge the mistake\n2. Generate proper furry/pop-art comic style images for all the articles\n3. Replace the gradient+silhouette images I created\n\nLet me look at the generate_blog_image.py to understand the furry/pop-art comic prompt style.",
